code for screening and we  do for diagnostic gnosis of clinical  infection  Oh  our next question is what testing  methodology incorporates perirectal  swabs for CDI testing  well right now the paragraph color which  means inter anal so you because the  rectum is actually inside the anus so  you do want to get the swab basically  the little white part at the end inside  of the anus none of the tests are  approved that the test that was used in  Canada in the study where they're short  of benefit was the expert but there was  no analytical validation of that in that  particular paper are the reason we  published our paper that I mentioned in  this in this report was again there's no  FDA cleared test for the Kobus 4,800 but  we wanted to validate it because we  wanted to do the screening test so we  validated the assay in our own  laboratory factually correspondent with  the Food and Drug Administration on this  and they were fine with that they said  once the test is cleared physicians have  a broad broad range of what they're  allowed to do with the test and as long  as we've validated the assay in our  laboratory that they would not take any  action to stop our laboratory from using  acid  and dr. Peterson it looks like we have  time for one more question do you  consider it economically justifiable  using zinc vancomycin Orthodox medicine  in the initial management of toxin  negative but severely symptomatic dgh  positive patients  of the gdh glutamate dehydrogenase so so  about 20% of the c.difficile strains  will will be positive by the gdh assay  and that's because it's part of their  cell structure and they don't produce  toxin so we would not recommend using  either fidex in Iceland or vancomycin in  a patient that was toxin negative now it  depends how you're doing the toxin test  I think that the enzyme immunoassays for  toxins are our cells to have a  sensitivity in the 60 to 70 percent  range so you could miss them but if  you're doing a gdh screening and then  you have a negative PCR test I think  that's pretty good evidence that you  have a nontoxigenic strain it would be  unusual to have both toxigenic and  nontoxigenic strain as president in the  stool I suppose it could happen but it  would be very unusual so I if I had a if  I was doing screening where the gdh test  had a positive test and then had done a  PCR test and it was negative I would not  I'd look for another reason for the  diarrhea and our gastroenterologist SAR  pretty helpful in that they've agreed to  actually do colonoscopy on patients  where there's a question it doesn't come  up very often but I think what you'd  really want to do is rather than is  rather than treating for a disease that  you probably don't think is really there  you'd get some help from the specialist  and the gastroenterologist either  examine the patient or do a colonoscopy  thank you again dr. Peterson do you have  any final comments for our audience I  appreciate the opportunity to talk about  this this is a interesting disease that  I've been working on since early in my  career for probably 40 years and it  still continues to emerge it is really a  challenge as you saw early on in the  presentation this disease is not getting  better  Illinois requires reporting of this  particular infection and I think that  data that's available online if you  google the Illinois report cards you can  look at the data through 2016 and you  can see that in Illinois that the rates  are continuing to rise mrsn rates are  slowly either levelling offered or going  down in Illinois but c.difficile  continues to go up so I think that this  is going to be a disease that will  evolve the laboratory for years to come  and it's important for the laboratory to  be involved in the control efforts along  with infection control  before we go I'd like to thank the  audience for joining us today and for  their interesting question  just a reminder any questions we did not  have time for today by the